      "rationale": "For unequal-mass binaries ($q < 1$), companions reach the sound speed at different times, causing two local eccentricity minima along an orbit rather than one, delaying late-time eccentricity growth and rapid orbital tightening.",
      "supporting_papers": [
        "Eytan_2025_arXiv_2509.15632v1.pdf"
      ],
      "testable_prediction": "Simulations of unequal-mass inspirals will show two distinct eccentricity minima linked to asynchronous transonic passages, with measurably prolonged inspiral phases compared to equal-mass systems.",
      "caveats": "Restricted strictly to linear response theory; ignores non-linear effects like strong shocks, accretion, and non-linear wake dynamics."
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "The geometric and kinematic parameters governing late-stage CE gas dynamical friction—specifically aspect ratios $B/A, C/A$, normalized semi-major axis $A/a$, and lag angle $\\Delta\\phi$—converge to universal values set primarily by the particle Mach number ($M_p$) independently of initial primary mass.",
      "rationale": "Time-averaged parameters found in equal-mass CE simulations match nearly identically with those extracted from binary supermassive black hole simulations despite vastly different astrophysical environments.",
      "supporting_papers": [
        "Bhattacharyya_et_al_2025_PASA.pdf"
      ],
      "testable_prediction": "Extracting wake geometry parameters across diverse primary masses with $q=1$ will yield invariant aspect ratios and lag angles at fixed subsonic Mach numbers.",
      "caveats": "Derived specifically for equal-mass ($q=1$) systems; may not directly apply to unequal-mass configurations."
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "For unequal-mass common envelope systems ($q \\neq 1$), the net gravitational drag torque deviates from prolate-spheroid symmetry into a pear-shaped wake configuration, but can be modeled analytically by superimposing two distinct, mass-weighted lagging spheroids centered on each component.",
      "rationale": "Individual wakes dominate local torques near each particle in simulations, and existing linear double-perturber formulations natively incorporate mass ratios.",
      "supporting_papers": [
        "Bhattacharyya_et_al_2025_PASA.pdf"
      ],
      "testable_prediction": "Superimposed mass-weighted lagging spheroid models will accurately reproduce torque magnitudes and phase oscillations in unequal-mass hydrodynamic CE simulations.",
      "caveats": "Assumes linear superposition holds adequately despite complex asymmetric density gradients."
    }
  ]
